ikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/USS_Midway_Museum.jpg/960px-USS_Midway_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Unconditional Surrender Monument","g":"sights","k":"artwork","la":32.712904,"lo":-117.175165,"d":361},{"n":"Museum of Contemporary Art","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":32.717247,"lo":-117.169406,"d":451,"h":"Th-Tu 11:00-17:00","w":"https://www.mcasd.org/","ph":"+1-858-454-3541"},{"n":"Star of India","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.720272,"lo":-117.17356,"d":476,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-16:00","w":"https://sdmaritime.org/visit/the-ships/star-of-india/","ph":"+1-619-234-9153","wp":"en:Star of India (ship)","ds":"Star of India is an iron-hulled sailing ship, built in 1863 in Ramsey, Isle of Man, as the full-rigged ship Euterpe. After a career sailing from Great Britain to India and New Zealand, she was renamed, re-rigged as a barque, and became a salmon hauler on the Alaska to California route.","wpu":"Star of India (ship)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Starofindia.jpg/960px-Starofindia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"HMS Surprise","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.720744,"lo":-117.173772,"d":527,"wp":"en:HMS Surprise (replica ship)","ds":"HMS Surprise is a modern tall ship built at Lunenburg, Nova Scotia, Canada. The vessel was built in 1970 as HMS Rose to a Phil Bolger design based on the original 18th-century British Admiralty drawings of HMS Rose, a 20-gun sixth-rate post ship from 1757.","wpu":"HMS Surprise (replica ship)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/HMS-Surprise-overall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Berkeley","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.720949,"lo":-117.174,"d":550,"wp":"en:Berkeley (ferryboat)","ds":"Berkeley was one of several ferryboats of the Southern Pacific Railroad that for sixty years operated on San Francisco Bay between the Oakland Pier and the San Francisco Ferry Building. Built in 1898 by the Union Iron Works of San Francisco, she served after the 1906 earthquake, ferrying refugees across the bay to Oakland.","wpu":"Berkeley (ferryboat)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Berkeley_Ferry_and_U.S.S._Dolphin_at_the_Maritime_Museum_of_San_Diego.jpg/960px-Berkeley_Ferry_and_U.S.S._Dolphin_at_the_Maritime_Museum_of_San_Diego.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Medea","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.721052,"lo":-117.17423,"d":562,"wp":"en:Medea (yacht)","ds":"The Medea is a 1904 steam yacht preserved in the Maritime Museum of San Diego, United States. Named after Medea, the wife of Jason, she was built in a record 51 days on the Clyde at Alexander Stephen and Sons shipyard at Linthouse by John Stephen for William Macalister Hall of Torrisdale Castle, Scotland.","wpu":"Medea (yacht)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/SDMM--Medea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"San Salvador","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.721025,"lo":-117.174682,"d":562,"wp":"en:San Salvador (Cabrillo's ship)","ds":"San Salvador was the flagship of explorer Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo (João Rodrigues Cabrilho in Portuguese). She was a 100-foot (30 m) full-rigged galleon with 10-foot (3.0 m) draft and capacity of 200 tons. She carried officers, crew, and a priest.","wpu":"San Salvador (Cabrillo's ship)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/San_Salvador_Replica_in_San_Diego.jpg/960px-San_Salvador_Replica_in_San_Diego.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"USS Dolphin","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.72107,"lo":-117.173763,"d":564,"wp":"en:USS Dolphin (AGSS-555)","ds":"USS Dolphin (AGSS-555) was a United States Navy diesel-electric deep-diving research and development submarine. She was commissioned in 1968 and decommissioned in 2007. Her 38-year career was the longest in history for a US Navy submarine to that point.","wpu":"USS Dolphin (AGSS-555)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/USS_Dolphin_AGSS-555.jpg/960px-USS_Dolphin_AGSS-555.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Californian","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.72102,"lo":-117.175192,"d":569,"wp":"en:Californian (schooner)","ds":"Californian is a 1984 replica of the United States Revenue Marine cutter Lawrence, which operated off the coast of California in the 1850s. On July 23, 2003, Governor Arnold Schwarzenegger signed Bill No. 965, making her the \"official state tall ship\" of California.","wpu":"Californian (schooner)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Bateaugoelette.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"The Guild Hotel, San Diego, a Tribute Portfolio Hotel","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":32.716017,"lo":-117.167856,"d":574,"w":"https://www.theguildhotel.com/","ph":"+1-619-795-6000","wifi":1},{"n":"San Diego Police Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":32.71069,"lo":-117.170511,"d":674,"w":"http://www.sdpolicemuseum.com/THE-MUSEUM.html"},{"n":"Benito Juarez","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":32.712748,"lo":-117.167842,"d":680},{"n":"Hotel Republic San Diego, Autograph Collection","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":32.717527,"lo":-117.166791,"d":695,"w":"https://www.marriott.com/en-us/hotels/sanya-hotel-republic-san-diego-autograph-collection/overview/","ph":"+1-619-398-3100","wifi":1},{"n":"San Diego Police Department Exhibit & Jail Cells","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":32.710589,"lo":-117.170109,"d":703,"h":"Mo-Sa 10:00-21:00, Su 10:00-20:00","w":"https://theheadquarters.com/police-jail-cells"},{"n":"Aaron Chang Ocean Art Gallery","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":32.71044,"lo":-117.170369,"d":705},{"n":"Mistletoe","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":32.708787